From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 23:07:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: HFR VRR, frame-sequential 3D on a 2D display with game VRR

CoreELEC shows 3D only on a sink that declares HDMI 3D: support_3d gates
the MVC demux, the stereo-mode switch and the video layer's 3D modes. A
current TV declares none, so 3D files play in 2D (an MVC remux decodes its
base view only), even though the box can show 3D frame-sequentially for
active shutter glasses on any display with game VRR.

Add a stereoscopic mode for it, HFR VRR, offered next to the others (Same
as movie, Over/Under, Side by side) where the platform supports it. In it
the GUI is drawn once, the same for both eyes, and only the video
alternates them. The other modes keep their usual meaning.

On Amlogic it is supported with no HDMI 3D, game VRR (vrr_min, as the
kernel tests it), a 1080p 120 Hz mode and a kernel that reports the video
layer's eye per vsync. In it:
- the sink stays in 2D: 1080p 120 Hz (its 119.88 Hz twin for a 1000/1001
  film rate) with ALLM and game VRR slowed to the largest even multiple of
  the frame rate (24p at 96 Hz, 23.976 at 95.904, every frame twice to
  each eye). Entering counts once the mode switch succeeded; a failed one
  plays the film in 2D. Leaving always runs, also after the TV went away,
  and a Kodi that died in it is cleaned up at the next start;
- the video layer alternates the eyes from the source's own layout (side
  by side, top and bottom, MVC) once the output has switched, and the film
  is not shown before that;
- the eyes swap for a right-eye-first source and for Kodi's "Swap
  left/right eye", also mid-playback: MVC in the decoder (view_mode), the
  others through the kernel's fa_swap_eyes, which the glasses follow;
- it is on exactly while a 3D film plays in it: the video codec asks for
  the output switch whenever that changes, or the film rate does (also
  mid-playback, and whatever the refresh rate setting), and the window
  system asks again when the player announces it stopped; the request
  reads Kodi's resolution when it is handled; the
  window stands while Kodi asks again for the resolution and film rate it
  was entered with, and other stereo mode changes do not re-sync a sink
  without HDMI 3D; quitting Kodi leaves it;
- a TV re-syncing its HDMI link keeps the capability it had, so a film
  does not drop out of 3D while it does;
- the video layer's 3D state is written when it changes, also when the
  output switches, and closing the decoder takes it back to 2D.

Shared code, kept to what a new stereo mode needs: the mode, its name in the
JSON-RPC schema and its label; the GUI drawn once in it; subtitles at screen
depth in it, and an eye of a packed stereo bitmap subtitle scaled against
one eye of the video, which the renderer does not crop to an eye.
DVDDemuxFFmpeg and StereoscopicsManager: the checks CoreELEC's AML code put
there widen from aml_display_support_3d to aml_display_support_stereo.

Without HDMI 3D, stereo video is taken as stereo (both MVC views, the
3D mode question, the preferred mode) only for a user who has chosen
HFR VRR, as the preferred or the current mode: on a 2D TV with game VRR
everyone else plays 3D files as 2D, as before. A kept HFR VRR re-applies
a new film's rate. A Kodi that died in 3D leaves, on the next start, the
video layer's frame alternation and eye swap off as well as VRR (checked
once, on the first refresh, so a hotplug while entering 3D cannot take
it down). The demuxer decides at open whether to read MVC's second view:
choosing HFR VRR from the OSD during a film that opened in 2D gives 3D
from the next film; with HFR VRR as the preferred mode, from the first.
One eye
of a packed subtitle is compared with one eye of the video in HFR VRR
only. JSON-RPC 13.1001.0: the stereoscopic mode enum has hfr_vrr.

A stereo mode changed while the film is paused never entered or left HFR VRR:
only the codec's SetVideoRect asked for the switch, and it runs on a new
frame. The window system now watches the GUI stereo mode on every pass of the
render loop (MessagePump) and asks for the switch itself when the mode
changes and HFR VRR is not as it should be. Once the output has switched, the
AML renderer hands the geometry to the last codec again, so the video layer
takes its 3D mode without waiting for a frame either.

CloseDecoder put the video layer back to 2D while the renderer could still be
inside SetVideoRect on the main thread for a frame it had found open, which
could set the frame-sequential mode again after the reset; the fields holding
that state were written by both threads without a lock. SetVideoRect now runs
under a mutex of its own and returns when the codec is closed to it;
CloseDecoder closes it and resets the 3D state under that mutex, and the 3D
state is only touched there.

A failed entry into HFR VRR now holds for the film playing only (a flag
cleared when playback stops) rather than switching frame-sequential 3D off
until the next hotplug, after which every 3D film played in 2D with no word
and MVC was no longer assembled. Between back-to-back 3D films, where the
player plays and the next film's decoder has not opened or opened on a stereo
layout, the 3D check is asked again every 200 ms for
up to 5 s instead of leaving 3D, which cost the sink two re-syncs and a mode
switch under the next film's decoder opening. The renderer keeps the codec it
last gave the geometry to across a Reset (a weak pointer, and SetVideoRect
refuses a closed codec), and counts a 3D change as seen only once a codec has
been told: a change during a paused flush otherwise left the video layer
disabled, black, until playback resumed. The sink's HDMI 3D flag is atomic,
since the demux thread reads it.

The hold between back-to-back films is decided by the stereo layout the next
film's decoder opens on, not by its first picture: Kodi asks for 2D when the
renderer is configured for that picture, before it has read the layout, so
a picture had always arrived by the time of the check (seen on the box:
every 3D film of a playlist left 3D and entered it again). The AML codec
records, as OpenDecoder starts, whether the stream is stereo the way its
pictures get their layout (the stream's stereo mode, which GetPicture passes
on, and the file's video settings only when the stream has none), and forgets
it at CloseDecoder. A film decoded in software never opens an AML decoder and
is held for the 5 s at most, as is a 3D film Kodi then plays in 2D; so is a
2D the viewer asks for within 5 s of a decoder closed in 3D, since the hold
cannot tell it from Kodi's own turning the stereo mode off. When the next
film's decoder opens on a 2D layout the checks are still held, for up to
1.5 s: a check leaves at the mode the 3D film had, and Kodi's resolution
update, once the film shows, switches again to the mode it picks for it (seen
on the box: 2160p24, then 1080p24 half a second later). Held, that update
leaves in one switch; without one (refresh rate not adjusted) the check at
the bound leaves.

On a playlist the leave did not come through that check: Kodi turns the
stereo mode off as each film starts, until it has read the new film's
layout, and the window it re-creates for the new film's resolution asks for
2D. set_fs3d_resolution now keeps frame-sequential 3D on such a request
while a film follows one shown in 3D: an AML decoder closed in 3D less than
5 s ago (noted synchronously by CloseDecoder), the player is playing, and the
new film's decoder has not opened, or opened on a stereo layout. The base
mode is then set again without a forced switch, so an unchanged mode costs
the sink nothing.
A stop is not playing and leaves at once; a 2D film leaves at Kodi's
resolution update for it or within 1.5 s of its decoder opening, and a film
decoded in software at the 5 s bound.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
